## sn48.10
sn48.10:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five faculties. | T: “Monks, there are these five faculties.
sn48.10:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn48.10:1.3 | S: The faculties of faith, energy, mindfulness, immersion, and wisdom. | T: The faculty of conviction, the faculty of persistence, the faculty of mindfulness, the faculty of concentration, the faculty of discernment.
sn48.10:1.4 | S: And what is the faculty of faith? | T: “Now what is the faculty of conviction?
sn48.10:1.5 | S: It’s when a noble disciple has faith in the Realized One’s awakening: | T: There is the case where a monk, a disciple of the noble ones, has conviction, is convinced of the Tathāgata’s awakening:
sn48.10:1.6 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
sn48.10:1.7 | S: This is called the faculty of faith. | T: This is called the faculty of conviction.
sn48.10:2.1 | S: And what is the faculty of energy? | T: “And what is the faculty of persistence?
sn48.10:2.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple lives with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They’re strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: There is the case where a monk, a disciple of the noble ones, keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful mental qualities and taking on skillful mental qualities. He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful mental qualities.
sn48.10:2.3 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that bad, unskillful qualities don’t arise. | T: He generates desire, endeavors, arouses persistence, upholds & exerts his intent for the sake of the non-arising of evil, unskillful qualities that have not yet arisen…
sn48.10:2.4 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen are given up. | T: for the sake of the abandoning of evil, unskillful qualities that have arisen…
sn48.10:2.5 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities arise. | T: for the sake of the arising of skillful qualities that have not yet arisen…
sn48.10:2.6 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities that have arisen remain, are not lost, but increase, mature, and are fully developed. | T: (and) for the maintenance, non-confusion, increase, plenitude, development, & culmination of skillful qualities that have arisen.
sn48.10:2.7 | S: This is called the faculty of energy. | T: This is called the faculty of persistence.
sn48.10:3.1 | S: And what is the faculty of mindfulness? | T: “And what is the faculty of mindfulness?
sn48.10:3.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple is mindful. They have utmost mindfulness and alertness, and can remember and recall what was said and done long ago. | T: There is the case where a monk, a disciple of the noble ones, is mindful, is endowed with excellent proficiency in mindfulness, remembering & able to call to mind even things that were done & said long ago.
sn48.10:3.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: He remains focused on the body in & of itself—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn48.10:3.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: He remains focused on feelings in & of themselves… the mind in & of itself…
sn48.10:3.5 | S: mind … | T: (none)
sn48.10:3.6 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: mental qualities in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn48.10:3.7 | S: This is called the faculty of mindfulness. | T: This is called the faculty of mindfulness.
sn48.10:4.1 | S: And what is the faculty of immersion? | T: “And what is the faculty of concentration?
sn48.10:4.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple, relying on letting go, gains immersion, gains unification of mind. | T: There is the case where a monk, a disciple of the noble ones, making it his object to let go, attains concentration, attains singleness of mind.
sn48.10:4.3 | S: Quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, they enter and remain in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: Quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—he enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
sn48.10:4.4 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, they enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: With the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.
sn48.10:4.5 | S: And with the fading away of rapture, they enter and remain in the third absorption, where they meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: With the fading of rapture, he remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, and senses pleasure with the body. He enters & remains in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’
sn48.10:4.6 | S: With the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, they enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: With the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—he enters & remains in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
sn48.10:4.7 | S: This is called the faculty of immersion. | T: This is called the faculty of concentration.
sn48.10:5.1 | S: And what is the faculty of wisdom? | T: “And what is the faculty of discernment?
sn48.10:5.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple is wise. They have the wisdom of arising and passing away which is noble, penetrative, and leads to the complete ending of suffering. | T: There is the case where a monk, a disciple of the noble ones, is discerning, endowed with discernment of arising & passing away—noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
sn48.10:5.3 | S: They truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: He discerns, as it has come to be: ‘This is stress…This is the origination of stress…This is the cessation of stress…This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
sn48.10:5.4 | S: This is called the faculty of wisdom. | T: This is called the faculty of discernment.
sn48.10:5.5 | S: These are the five faculties.” | T: “These are the five faculties.”
sn48.10:5.6 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
sn48.10:5.7 | P: Suddhikavaggo paṭhamo. | T: (none)
sn48.10:6.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn48.10:6.1 | P: Suddhikañceva dve sotā, | T: (none)
sn48.10:6.2 | P: arahantā apare duve; | T: (none)
sn48.10:6.3 | P: Samaṇabrāhmaṇā daṭṭhabbaṁ, | T: (none)
sn48.10:6.4 | P: vibhaṅgā apare duveti. | T: (none)

