## sn12.63
sn12.63:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn12.63:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, these four fuels maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: “There are these four nutriments for the maintenance of beings who have come into being or for the support of those in search of a place to be born.
sn12.63:1.3 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
sn12.63:1.4 | S: Edible food, whether solid or subtle; contact is the second, mental intention the third, and consciousness the fourth. | T: Physical food, gross or refined; contact as the second, intellectual intention the third, and consciousness the fourth.
sn12.63:1.5 | S: These are the four fuels that maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: These are the four nutriments for the maintenance of beings who have come into being or for the support of those in search of a place to be born.
sn12.63:2.1 | S: And how should you regard edible food? | T: “And how is physical food to be regarded?
sn12.63:2.2 | S: Suppose a couple who were husband and wife set out to cross a desert, taking limited supplies. | T: Suppose a couple, husband & wife, taking meager provisions, were to travel through a desert.
sn12.63:2.3 | S: They had an only child, dear and beloved. | T: With them would be their only baby son, loved & endearing.
sn12.63:2.4 | S: As the couple were crossing the desert their limited quantity of supplies would run out, | T: Then the meager provisions of the couple going through the desert would be used up & depleted while there was still a stretch of the desert yet to be crossed.
sn12.63:2.5 | S: and they’d still have the rest of the desert to cross. | T: (none)
sn12.63:2.6 | S: Then it would occur to that couple: | T: The thought would occur to them,
sn12.63:2.7 | S: ‘Our limited quantity of supplies has run out, | T: ‘Our meager provisions are used up & depleted while there is still a stretch of this desert yet to be crossed.
sn12.63:2.8 | S: and we still have the rest of the desert to cross. | T: (none)
sn12.63:2.9 | S: Why don’t we kill our only child, so dear and beloved, and prepare dried and spiced meat? Then we can make it across the desert by eating our child’s flesh. Let not all three perish.’ | T: What if we were to kill this only baby son of ours, loved & endearing, and make dried meat & jerky? That way—chewing on the flesh of our son—at least the two of us would make it through this desert. Otherwise, all three of us would perish.’
sn12.63:2.10 | S: Then that couple would kill their only child, so dear and beloved, and prepare dried and spiced meat. They’d make it across the desert by eating their child’s flesh. | T: So they would kill their only baby son, loved & endearing, and make dried meat & jerky. Chewing on the flesh of their son, they would make it through the desert.
sn12.63:2.11 | S: And as they’d eat their child’s flesh, they’d beat their breasts and cry: | T: While eating the flesh of their only son, they would beat their breasts, (crying,)
sn12.63:2.12 | S: ‘Where are you, our only child? Where are you, our only child?’ | T: ‘Where have you gone, our only baby son? Where have you gone, our only baby son?’
sn12.63:3.1 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: Now what do you think, monks?
sn12.63:3.2 | S: Would they eat that food for fun, indulgence, adornment, or decoration?” | T: Would that couple eat that food playfully or for intoxication, or for putting on bulk, or for beautification?”
sn12.63:3.3 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn12.63:3.4 | S: “Wouldn’t they eat that food just so they could make it across the desert?” | T: “Wouldn’t they eat that food simply for the sake of making it through that desert?”
sn12.63:3.5 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
sn12.63:3.6 | S: “I say that this is how you should regard edible food. | T: “In the same way, I tell you, is the nutriment of physical food to be regarded.
sn12.63:3.7 | S: When edible food is completely understood, desire for the five kinds of sensual stimulation is completely understood. | T: When physical food is comprehended, passion for the five strings of sensuality is comprehended.
sn12.63:3.8 | S: When desire for the five kinds of sensual stimulation is completely understood, a noble disciple is bound by no fetter that might return them again to this world. | T: When passion for the five strings of sensuality is comprehended, there is no fetter bound by which a disciple of the noble ones would come back again to this world.
sn12.63:4.1 | S: And how should you regard contact as fuel? | T: “And how is the nutriment of contact to be regarded?
sn12.63:4.2 | S: Suppose there was a flayed cow. If she stands by a wall, the creatures on the wall bite her. | T: Suppose a flayed cow were to stand leaning against a wall. The creatures living in the wall would chew on it.
sn12.63:4.3 | S: If she stands under a tree, the creatures in the tree bite her. | T: If it were to stand leaning against a tree, the creatures living in the tree would chew on it.
sn12.63:4.4 | S: If she stands in some water, the creatures in the water bite her. | T: If it were to stand exposed to water, the creatures living in the water would chew on it.
sn12.63:4.5 | S: If she stands in the open, the creatures in the open bite her. | T: If it were to stand exposed to the air, the creatures living in the air would chew on it.
sn12.63:4.6 | S: Wherever that flayed cow stands, the creatures there would bite her. | T: For wherever the flayed cow were to stand exposed, the creatures living there would chew on it.
sn12.63:4.7 | S: I say that this is how you should regard contact as fuel. | T: In the same way, I tell you, is the nutriment of contact to be regarded.
sn12.63:4.8 | S: When contact as fuel is completely understood, the three feelings are completely understood. | T: When the nutriment of contact is comprehended, the three feelings [pleasure, pain, neither pleasure nor pain] are comprehended.
sn12.63:4.9 | S: When the three feelings are completely understood, a noble disciple has nothing further to do, I say. | T: When the three feelings are comprehended, I tell you, there is nothing further for a disciple of the noble ones to do.
sn12.63:5.1 | S: And how should you regard mental intention as fuel? | T: “And how is the nutriment of intellectual intention to be regarded?
sn12.63:5.2 | S: Suppose there was a pit of glowing coals deeper than a man’s height, filled with glowing coals that neither flamed nor smoked. | T: Suppose there were a pit of glowing embers, deeper than a man’s height, full of embers that were neither flaming nor smoking,
sn12.63:5.3 | S: Then a person would come along who wants to live and doesn’t want to die, who wants to be happy and recoils from pain. | T: and a man were to come along—loving life, hating death, loving pleasure, abhorring pain—
sn12.63:5.4 | S: Two strong men would grab them by the arms and drag them toward the pit of glowing coals. | T: and two strong men, having grabbed him by the arms, were to drag him to the pit of embers.
sn12.63:5.5 | S: Then that person’s intention, aim, and wish would be to get far away. | T: To get far away would be that man’s intention, far away would be his wish, far away would be his aspiration.
sn12.63:5.6 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn12.63:5.7 | S: Because that person would think: | T: Because he would realize,
sn12.63:5.8 | S: ‘If I fall in that pit of glowing coals, that will result in my death or deadly pain.’ | T: ‘If I fall into this pit of glowing embers, I will meet with death from that cause, or with death-like pain.’
sn12.63:5.9 | S: I say that this is how you should regard mental intention as fuel. | T: In the same way, I tell you, is the nutriment of intellectual intention to be regarded.
sn12.63:5.10 | S: When mental intention as fuel is completely understood, the three cravings are completely understood. | T: When the nutriment of intellectual intention is comprehended, the three forms of craving [for sensuality, for becoming, and for non-becoming] are comprehended.
sn12.63:5.11 | S: When the three cravings are completely understood, a noble disciple has nothing further to do, I say. | T: When the three forms of craving are comprehended, I tell you, there is nothing further for a disciple of the noble ones to do.
sn12.63:6.1 | S: And how should you regard consciousness as fuel? | T: “And how is the nutriment of consciousness to be regarded?
sn12.63:6.2 | S: Suppose they were to arrest a bandit, a criminal and present him to the king, saying: | T: Suppose that, having arrested a thief, a criminal, they were to show him to the king:
sn12.63:6.3 | S: ‘Your Majesty, this is a bandit, a criminal. Punish him as you will.’ | T: ‘This is a thief, a criminal for you, your majesty. Impose on him whatever punishment you like.’
sn12.63:6.4 | S: The king would say: | T: So the king would say,
sn12.63:6.5 | S: ‘Go, my men, and strike this man in the morning with a hundred spears!’ | T: ‘Go, men, and stab him in the morning with a hundred spears.’
sn12.63:6.6 | S: The king’s men did as they were told. | T: So they would stab him in the morning with a hundred spears.
sn12.63:6.7 | S: Then at midday the king would say: | T: Then the king would say at noon,
sn12.63:6.8 | S: ‘Look here, how is that man?’ | T: ‘Men, how is that man?’
sn12.63:6.9 | S: ‘He’s still alive, Your Majesty.’ | T: ‘Still alive, your majesty.’
sn12.63:6.10 | S: The king would say: | T: So the king would say,
sn12.63:6.11 | S: ‘Go, my men, and strike this man in the middle of the day with a hundred spears!’ | T: ‘Go, men, and stab him at noon with a hundred spears.’
sn12.63:6.12 | S: The king’s men did as they were told. | T: So they would stab him at noon with a hundred spears.
sn12.63:6.13 | S: Then late in the afternoon the king would say: | T: Then the king would say in the evening,
sn12.63:6.14 | S: ‘Look here, how is that man?’ | T: ‘Men, how is that man?’
sn12.63:6.15 | S: ‘He’s still alive, Your Majesty.’ | T: ‘Still alive, your majesty.’
sn12.63:6.16 | S: The king would say: | T: So the king would say,
sn12.63:6.17 | S: ‘Go, my men, and strike this man in the late afternoon with a hundred spears!’ | T: ‘Go, men, and stab him in the evening with a hundred spears.’
sn12.63:6.18 | S: The king’s men did as they were told. | T: So they would stab him in the evening with a hundred spears.
sn12.63:6.19 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: Now what do you think, monks?
sn12.63:6.20 | S: Would that man experience pain and distress from being struck with three hundred spears a day?” | T: Would that man, being stabbed with three hundred spears a day, experience pain & distress from that cause?”
sn12.63:6.21 | S: “Sir, that man would experience pain and distress from being struck with one spear, | T: “Even if he were to be stabbed with only one spear, lord, he would experience pain & distress from that cause,
sn12.63:6.22 | S: let alone three hundred spears!” | T: to say nothing of three hundred spears.”
sn12.63:6.23 | S: “I say that this is how you should regard consciousness as fuel. | T: “In the same way, I tell you, monks, is the nutriment of consciousness to be regarded.
sn12.63:6.24 | S: When consciousness as fuel is completely understood, name and form are completely understood. When name and form are completely understood, a noble disciple has nothing further to do, I say.” | T: When the nutriment of consciousness is comprehended, name-&-form is comprehended. When name-&-form is comprehended, I tell you, there is nothing further for a disciple of the noble ones to do.”
sn12.63:6.25 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.54
an7.54:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then a certain monk went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
an7.54:1.2 | S: “Sir, what is the cause, what is the reason why a learned noble disciple has no doubts regarding the undeclared points?” | T: “Lord, what is the cause, what is the reason, why uncertainty doesn’t arise in an instructed disciple of the noble ones over the undeclared issues?”
an7.54:2.1 | S: “Mendicant, it’s due to the cessation of views that a learned noble disciple has no doubts regarding the undeclared points. | T: “Because of the cessation of views, monk, uncertainty doesn’t arise in an instructed disciple of the noble ones over the undeclared issues.
an7.54:2.2 | S: ‘A realized one still exists after death’: this is a misconception. | T: The view-standpoint, ‘The Tathāgata exists after death,’
an7.54:2.3 | S: ‘A realized one no longer exists after death’: this is a misconception. | T: the view-standpoint, ‘The Tathāgata doesn’t exist after death,’
an7.54:2.4 | S: ‘A realized one both still exists and no longer exists after death’: this is a misconception. | T: the view-standpoint, ‘The Tathāgata both does and doesn’t exist after death,’
an7.54:2.5 | S: ‘A realized one neither still exists nor no longer exists after death’: this is a misconception. | T: the view-standpoint, ‘The Tathāgata neither does nor doesn’t exist after death’:
an7.54:2.6 | S: An unlearned ordinary person doesn’t understand views, their origin, their cessation, or the practice that leads to their cessation. | T: The uninstructed run-of-the-mill person doesn’t discern view, doesn’t discern the origination of view, doesn’t discern the cessation of view, doesn’t discern the path of practice leading to the cessation of view,
an7.54:2.7 | S: And so their views grow. They’re not freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. They’re not freed from suffering, I say. | T: and so for him that view grows. He is not freed from birth, aging, & death; from sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs. He is not freed, I tell you, from suffering & stress.
an7.54:3.1 | S: A learned noble disciple does understand views, their origin, their cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation. | T: But the instructed disciple of the noble ones discerns view, discerns the origination of view, discerns the cessation of view, discerns the path of practice leading to the cessation of view,
an7.54:3.2 | S: And so their views cease. They’re freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. They’re freed from suffering, I say. | T: and so for him that view ceases. He is freed from birth, aging, & death; from sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs. He is freed, I tell you, from suffering & stress.
an7.54:3.3 | S: Knowing and seeing this, a learned noble disciple does not answer: ‘A realized one still exists after death’, | T: “Thus knowing, thus seeing, the instructed disciple of the noble ones doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata exists after death,’
an7.54:3.4 | S: ‘A realized one no longer exists after death’, | T: doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata doesn’t exist after death,’
an7.54:3.5 | S: ‘A realized one both still exists and no longer exists after death’, | T: doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata both does and doesn’t exist after death,’
an7.54:3.6 | S: ‘A realized one neither still exists nor no longer exists after death.’ | T: doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata neither does nor doesn’t exist after death.’
an7.54:3.7 | S: Knowing and seeing this, a learned noble disciple does not declare the undeclared points. | T: Thus knowing, thus seeing, he is thus of a nature not to declare the undeclared issues.
an7.54:3.8 | S: Knowing and seeing this, a learned noble disciple doesn’t shake, tremble, quake, or get nervous regarding the undeclared points. | T: Thus knowing, thus seeing, he isn’t paralyzed, doesn’t quake, doesn’t shiver or shake over the undeclared issues.
an7.54:4.1 | S: ‘A realized one still exists after death’: this is just about craving. … | T: “‘The Tathāgata exists after death’—this craving-standpoint,
an7.54:4.2 | S: it’s just about perception … | T: this perception-standpoint,
an7.54:4.3 | S: it’s a conceiving … | T: this product of conceiving,
an7.54:4.4 | S: it’s a proliferation … | T: this product of elaboration,
an7.54:4.5 | S: it’s just about grasping … | T: this clinging-standpoint:
an7.54:4.6 | S: ‘A realized one still exists after death’: this is a regret. | T: That’s anguish.
an7.54:4.7 | S: ‘A realized one no longer exists after death’: this is a regret. | T: ‘The Tathāgata doesn’t exist after death’: That’s anguish.
an7.54:4.8 | S: ‘A realized one both still exists and no longer exists after death’: this is a regret. | T: ‘The Tathāgata both does and doesn’t exist after death’: That’s anguish.
an7.54:4.9 | S: ‘A realized one neither still exists nor no longer exists after death’: this is a regret. | T: ‘The Tathāgata neither does nor doesn’t exist after death’: That’s anguish.
an7.54:4.10 | S: An unlearned ordinary person doesn’t understand regrets, their origin, their cessation, or the practice that leads to their cessation. | T: The uninstructed run-of-the-mill person doesn’t discern anguish, doesn’t discern the origination of anguish, doesn’t discern the cessation of anguish, doesn’t discern the path of practice leading to the cessation of anguish,
an7.54:4.11 | S: And so their regrets grow. They’re not freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. They’re not freed from suffering, I say. | T: and so for him that anguish grows. He is not freed from birth, aging, & death; from sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs. He is not freed, I tell you, from suffering & stress.
an7.54:5.1 | S: A learned noble disciple does understand regrets, their origin, their cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation. | T: But the instructed disciple of the noble ones discerns anguish, discerns the origination of anguish, discerns the cessation of anguish, discerns the path of practice leading to the cessation of anguish,
an7.54:5.2 | S: And so their regrets cease. They’re freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. They’re freed from suffering, I say. | T: and so for him that anguish ceases. He is freed from birth, aging, & death; from sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs. He is freed, I tell you, from suffering & stress.
an7.54:5.3 | S: Knowing and seeing this, a learned noble disciple does not answer: ‘A realized one still exists after death’ … | T: “Thus knowing, thus seeing, the instructed disciple of the noble ones doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata exists after death,’ doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata doesn’t exist after death,’ doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata both does and doesn’t exist after death,’
an7.54:5.4 | S: ‘A realized one neither still exists nor no longer exists after death.’ | T: doesn’t declare that ‘The Tathāgata neither does nor doesn’t exist after death.’
an7.54:5.5 | S: Knowing and seeing this, a learned noble disciple does not declare the undeclared points. | T: Thus knowing, thus seeing, he is thus of a nature not to declare the undeclared issues.
an7.54:5.6 | S: Knowing and seeing this, a learned noble disciple doesn’t shake, tremble, quake, or get nervous regarding the undeclared points. | T: Thus knowing, thus seeing, he isn’t paralyzed, doesn’t quake, doesn’t shiver or shake over the undeclared issues.”
an7.54:5.7 | S: This is the cause, this is the reason why a learned noble disciple has no doubts regarding the undeclared points.” | T: (none)
an7.54:5.8 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.60
an3.60:1.1 | S: Then Saṅgārava the brahmin went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the brahman Saṅgārava went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an3.60:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an3.60:1.3 | S: “Worthy Gotama, we who are called brahmins | T: “I say, Master Gotama. We brahmans
an3.60:1.4 | S: make sacrifices and encourage others to make sacrifices. | T: perform sacrifices and get others to perform sacrifices.
an3.60:1.5 | S: Now, worthy Gotama, both of these people—the one who sacrifices and the one who encourages others to sacrifice—are doing good for many people on account of that sacrifice. | T: And whoever performs a sacrifice, whoever gets others to perform a sacrifice, they have all practiced a practice of merit—the business of a sacrifice—(that benefits) countless beings.
an3.60:1.6 | S: But, worthy Gotama, when someone has gone forth from the lay life to homelessness, they tame, calm, and extinguish themselves alone. That being so, they are doing good for just one person on account of that going forth.” | T: But whoever, leaving his family, has gone forth from the home life into homelessness, and tames his single self, brings his single self into tune, brings his single self to unbinding: his practice of merit—this business of going forth—is one (that benefits) only one being.”
an3.60:2.1 | S: “Well then, brahmin, I’ll ask you about this in return, and you can answer as you like. | T: “Very well then, brahman, in that case I will cross-question you. Answer as you see fit.
an3.60:2.2 | S: What do you think, brahmin? | T: What do you think?
an3.60:2.3 | S: A Realized One arises in the world, perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed. | T: There is the case where a Tathāgata appears in the world, a worthy one, rightly-self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, Well-Gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.
an3.60:2.4 | S: He says, | T: He says:
an3.60:2.5 | S: ‘Come, this is the path, this is the practice. Practicing like this, I realized the supreme objective of the spiritual life with my own insight, and I make it known. | T: ‘Here! This is the path, this is the practice which, having practiced, I make known the unexcelled gaining of a footing in the holy life, having directly known & realized it for myself.
an3.60:2.6 | S: Please, all of you, practice like this, and you too will realize the supreme objective of the spiritual life, and will live having realized it with your own insight.’ | T: Come! You, too, practice in such a way that you will remain in the unexcelled gaining of a footing in the holy life, having directly known & realized it for yourselves.’
an3.60:2.7 | S: So the Teacher teaches Dhamma, and others practice accordingly, in their hundreds and thousands, and hundreds of thousands. | T: Thus the Teacher teaches the Dhamma, and others practice, for authenticity [tathattā]. And there are countless hundreds of them, countless thousands of them, countless hundreds of thousands of them.
an3.60:3.1 | S: What do you think, brahmin? | T: (none)
an3.60:3.2 | S: This being so, are they doing good for just one person or for many people on account of going forth?” | T: This being the case, is this practice of merit—this business of going-forth—one that benefits countless beings, or only one being?”
an3.60:3.3 | S: “This being so, worthy Gotama, they are doing good for many people on account of going forth.” | T: “This being the case, Master Gotama, this practice of merit—this business of going-forth—is one that benefits countless beings.”
an3.60:4.1 | S: When he said this, Venerable Ānanda said to Saṅgārava, | T: When this was said, Ven. Ānanda said to the brahman Saṅgārava,
an3.60:4.2 | S: “Brahmin, which of these two practices do you believe has fewer obligations and undertakings, yet is more fruitful and beneficial?” | T: “Of these two practices, brahman, which appeals to you as the less complicated, the less violent, the more fruitful, & the more rewarding?”
an3.60:4.3 | S: Saṅgārava said to Ānanda, | T: When this was said, the brahman Saṅgārava said to Ven. Ānanda,
an3.60:4.4 | S: “Those such as the worthies Gotama and Ānanda | T: “Just as with Master Gotama & Master Ānanda,
an3.60:4.5 | S: are honored and praised by me!” | T: I worship them, I praise them (both).”
an3.60:5.1 | S: For a second time, Ānanda said to Saṅgārava, | T: A second time, Ven. Ānanda said to him,
an3.60:5.2 | S: “Brahmin, I didn’t ask you | T: “I didn’t ask you whom you worship and whom you praise.
an3.60:5.3 | S: who you honor and praise. | T: (none)
an3.60:5.4 | S: I asked you | T: I ask you,
an3.60:5.5 | S: which of these two practices do you believe has fewer obligations and undertakings, yet is more fruitful and beneficial?” | T: ‘Of these two practices, brahman, which appeals to you as the less complicated, the less violent, the more fruitful, & the more rewarding?’”
an3.60:5.6 | S: For a second time Saṅgārava said to Ānanda, | T: A second time, the brahman Saṅgārava said to Ven. Ānanda,
an3.60:5.7 | S: “Those such as the worthies Gotama and Ānanda | T: “Just as with Master Gotama & Master Ānanda,
an3.60:5.8 | S: are honored and praised by me!” | T: I worship them, I praise them (both).”
an3.60:6.1 | S: For a third time, Ānanda said to Saṅgārava, | T: A third time, Ven. Ānanda said to him,
an3.60:6.2 | S: “Brahmin, I didn’t ask you | T: “I didn’t ask you whom you worship and whom you praise.
an3.60:6.3 | S: who you honor and praise. | T: (none)
an3.60:6.4 | S: I asked you | T: I ask you,
an3.60:6.5 | S: which of these two practices do you believe has fewer obligations and undertakings, yet is more fruitful and beneficial?” | T: ‘Of these two practices, brahman, which appeals to you as the less complicated, the less violent, the more fruitful, & the more rewarding?’”
an3.60:6.6 | S: For a third time Saṅgārava said to Ānanda, | T: A third time, the brahman Saṅgārava said to Ven. Ānanda,
an3.60:6.7 | S: “Those such as the worthies Gotama and Ānanda | T: “Just as with Master Gotama & Master Ānanda,
an3.60:6.8 | S: are honored and praised by me!” | T: I worship them, I praise them (both).”
an3.60:7.1 | S: Then it occurred to the Buddha, | T: Then the thought occurred to the Blessed One,
an3.60:7.2 | S: “Though Ānanda asked him a sensible question three times, Saṅgārava falters without answering. | T: “Being asked a legitimate question by Ānanda up to the third time, the brahman Saṅgārava evades it and does not reply to it.
an3.60:7.3 | S: Why don’t I give him a way out?” | T: Suppose I were to get him out (of this dilemma).”
an3.60:7.4 | S: So the Buddha said to Saṅgārava, | T: So the Blessed One said to the brahman Saṅgārava,
an3.60:7.5 | S: “Brahmin, what came up in the conversation among the king’s retinue today, sitting together in the royal compound?” | T: “Brahman, what was the topic of conversation that arose today when the royal court sat gathered in the royal palace?”
an3.60:7.6 | S: “Worthy Gotama, this came up: | T: “Master Gotama, this was the topic of conversation that arose today when the royal court sat gathered in the royal palace:
an3.60:7.7 | S: ‘Formerly, it seems, there were fewer mendicants, but more of them displayed superhuman demonstrations of psychic power; | T: ‘In the past, there were fewer monks but more who, endowed with superior human attainments, displayed the miracle of psychic power.
an3.60:7.8 | S: while these days, there are more mendicants, but fewer display superhuman demonstrations of psychic power.’ | T: Now there are more monks but fewer who, endowed with superior human attainments, display the miracle of psychic power.
an3.60:7.9 | S: This is what came up in the conversation among the king’s retinue today, while sitting together in the royal compound.” | T: This, Master Gotama, was the topic of conversation that arose today when the royal court sat gathered in the royal palace.”
an3.60:8.1 | S: “Brahmin, there are three kinds of demonstration. | T: “Brahman, there are these three miracles.
an3.60:8.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.60:8.3 | S: A demonstration of psychic power, a demonstration of revealing, and a demonstration of instruction. | T: The miracle of psychic power, the miracle of telepathy, & the miracle of instruction.
an3.60:8.4 | S: And what is the demonstration of psychic power? | T: “And what is the miracle of psychic power?
an3.60:8.5 | S: It’s when someone wields the many kinds of psychic power: multiplying themselves and becoming one again; materializing and dematerializing; going unobstructed through a wall, a rampart, or a mountain as if through space; diving in and out of the earth as if it were water; walking on water as if it were earth; flying cross-legged through the sky like a bird; touching and stroking with the hand the sun and moon, so mighty and powerful. They control the body as far as the realm of divinity. | T: There is the case where a certain person wields manifold psychic powers. Having been one he becomes many; having been many he becomes one. He appears. He vanishes. He goes unimpeded through walls, ramparts, & mountains as if through space. He dives in and out of the earth as if it were water. He walks on water without sinking as if it were dry land. Sitting cross-legged he flies through the air like a winged bird. With his hand he touches and strokes even the sun & moon, so mighty & powerful. He exercises influence with his body even as far as the Brahmā worlds.
an3.60:8.6 | S: This is called the demonstration of psychic power. | T: This is called the miracle of psychic power.
an3.60:9.1 | S: And what is the demonstration of revealing? | T: “And what is the miracle of telepathy?
an3.60:9.2 | S: In one case, someone reveals by means of a sign: | T: There is the case where a certain person reads (another person’ thoughts) by means of a sign [vision], (saying,)
an3.60:9.3 | S: ‘This is what you’re thinking, such is your thought, and thus is your state of mind.’ | T: ‘Such is your thinking, here is where your thinking is, thus is your mind.’
an3.60:9.4 | S: And even if they reveal this many times, it turns out exactly so, not otherwise. | T: And however much he may read, that’s exactly how it is, and not otherwise.
an3.60:10.1 | S: In another case, someone reveals after hearing it from humans or non-humans or deities: | T: “Then there is the case where a certain person reads (another person’s thoughts), not by means of a sign or vision, but by hearing the voice of human beings, non-human beings, or devas, (saying,)
an3.60:10.2 | S: ‘This is what you’re thinking, such is your thought, and thus is your state of mind.’ | T: ‘Such is your thinking, here is where your thinking is, thus is your mind.’
an3.60:10.3 | S: And even if they reveal this many times, it turns out exactly so, not otherwise. | T: And however much he may read, that’s exactly how it is, and not otherwise.
an3.60:11.1 | S: In another case, someone reveals by hearing the sound of thought spreading as someone thinks and considers: | T: “Then there is the case where a certain person reads (another person’s thoughts), not by means of a sign or vision; not by hearing the voice of human beings, non-human beings, or devas; but by hearing the sound of the directed thought & evaluation of a person thinking directed thoughts and evaluating, (saying,)
an3.60:11.2 | S: ‘This is what you’re thinking, such is your thought, and thus is your state of mind.’ | T: ‘Such is your thinking, here is where your thinking is, thus is your mind.’
an3.60:11.3 | S: And even if they reveal this many times, it turns out exactly so, not otherwise. | T: And however much he may read, that’s exactly how it is, and not otherwise.
an3.60:12.1 | S: In another case, someone comprehends the mind of one who has attained the immersion that’s free of placing the mind and keeping it connected. They understand: | T: “Then there is the case where a certain person reads (another person’s thoughts), not by means of a sign or vision; not by hearing the voice of human beings, non-human beings, or devas; not by hearing the sound of the directed thought & evaluation of a person thinking directed thoughts and evaluating; but by encompassing with his own awareness the awareness of one who has attained a concentration devoid of directed thought & evaluation, he discerns,
an3.60:12.2 | S: ‘Judging by the way this worthy’s intentions are directed, immediately after this mind state, they’ll think this thought.’ | T: ‘Given the way the mental fabrications of this venerable person are inclined, the directed thoughts of his mind will immediately think about this.’
an3.60:12.3 | S: And even if they reveal this many times, it turns out exactly so, not otherwise. | T: And however much he may read, that’s exactly how it is, and not otherwise.
an3.60:12.4 | S: This is called the demonstration of revealing. | T: “This, brahman, is the miracle of telepathy.
an3.60:13.1 | S: And what is a demonstration of instruction? | T: “And what is the miracle of instruction?
an3.60:13.2 | S: It’s when someone instructs others like this: | T: There is the case where a certain person gives instruction in this way:
an3.60:13.3 | S: ‘Think like this, not like that. Focus your mind like this, not like that. Give up this, and live having achieved that.’ | T: ‘Direct your thought in this way, don’t direct it in that. Attend to things in this way, don’t attend to them in that. Let go of this, enter and remain in that.’
an3.60:13.4 | S: This is called a demonstration of instruction. | T: This is called the miracle of instruction.
an3.60:13.5 | S: These are the three kinds of demonstration. | T: “And these are the three miracles.
an3.60:13.6 | S: Of these three kinds of demonstration, which do you consider to be the finest?” | T: “Now, brahman, of these three miracles, which one appeals to you as the highest & most sublime?”
an3.60:14.1 | S: “Regarding this, worthy Gotama, a demonstration of psychic power | T: “Master Gotama, of these three miracles, the miracle of psychic power where a certain person wields manifold psychic powers…
an3.60:14.2 | P: yāva brāhmalokāpi kāyena vasaṁ vatteti, | T: (and) exercises influence with his body even as far as the Brahmā worlds:
an3.60:14.3 | S: is experienced only by the one who performs it, occurring only to them. | T: That is a miracle experienced only by him who does it; it belongs only to him who does it.
an3.60:14.4 | S: This seems to me like an illusion. | T: It seems to me to be of the nature of an illusion.
an3.60:15.1 | S: And the demonstration where someone reveals something by means of a sign, | T: “As for the miracle where a certain person by means of a sign or vision…
an3.60:15.2 | P: ‘evampi te mano, itthampi te mano, itipi te cittan’ti, so bahuñcepi ādisati tatheva taṁ hoti, no aññathā. | T: (none)
an3.60:15.3 | S: or after hearing it from humans, non-humans, or deities, | T: by hearing the voice of human beings, non-human beings, or devas…
an3.60:15.4 | S: or by hearing the sound of thought spreading as someone thinks and considers, | T: by hearing the sound of the directed thought & evaluation of a person thinking directed thoughts and evaluating, (saying,) ‘Such is your thinking, here is where your thinking is, thus is your mind.’ …
an3.60:15.5 | S: or by comprehending the mind of another, | T: (or) who by encompassing with his own awareness the awareness of one who has attained a concentration devoid of directed thought & evaluation, he discerns,
an3.60:15.6 | P: ‘yathā imassa bhoto manosaṅkhārā paṇihitā imassa cittassa anantarā amhaṁ nāma vitakkaṁ vitakkessatī’ti, so bahuñcepi ādisati tatheva taṁ hoti, no aññathā. | T: ‘Given the way the mental fabrications of this venerable person are inclined, the directed thoughts of his mind will immediately think about this.’ And however much he may read, that’s exactly how it is, and not otherwise:

